    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d447">

                <pb facs="0385" n="373" sameAs="frus:frus1942v04/pg373"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">882.7962/72: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Chargé in
                        Liberia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Wharton</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-03-26T20:00:00-04:00">March 26, 1942—8
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>70. Your 96, March 22, 2 p.m.<anchor n="19" xml:id="d447-f1"/> For McBride:
                    Secretary of War has now signed letter approving agreement, subject to
                    modifications described in Department’s 68, March 25, 9 p.m. You may therefore
                    proceed immediately to final action.</p>
                <p>The War Department also concurs in your proposed confidential letter to President
                        Barclay<anchor n="20" xml:id="d447-f2"/> with the exception of items 4 and
                    5. It suggests the replacement of these items by the following: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <list>
                            <item>“4. The extension of a credit in the sum of $800,000 from
                                Lend-Lease or other United States funds for the purpose of assisting
                                in the road construction and defense program of Liberia.</item>
                            <item>5. A supply of small arms and ammunition for a force of two to
                                three thousand men to be made available to the Liberian Government
                                by the United States as a part of supplies to be furnished under the
                                credit above mentioned.</item>
                            <item>6. The construction by the United States of access roads to areas
                                required by the American defense forces in Liberia.”</item>
                        </list>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <p>At the risk of repetition, it should once again be emphasized that in addition to
                    the $800,000 mentioned in item 4, the Ferrying Command is spending $200,000 for
                    the construction of the most important access roads. The total constitutes the
                    so-called million dollar program of assistance to Liberia. You are authorized to
                    amend the letter so as to make this clear if you consider it advisable.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Welles</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
